WEBVTT

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:10.868
We will total three item counts with two fresh stream

00:00:10.868 --> 00:00:11.514
pipelines.

00:00:11.514 --> 00:00:14.674
The goal is the same correct sum whether the work

00:00:14.674 --> 00:00:17.125
stays together or is split into parts.

00:00:31.124 --> 00:00:34.004
Reduction combines elements into one result.

00:00:34.004 --> 00:00:37.408
Reduce uses zero as its identity: a neutral starting

00:00:37.408 --> 00:00:39.633
value that leaves a sum unchanged.

00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:41.924
The lambda combines left and right.

00:00:41.924 --> 00:00:45.001
Its two Integer inputs and Integer result match

00:00:45.001 --> 00:00:48.208
BinaryOperator, the functional interface for this

00:00:48.208 --> 00:00:48.993
combination.

00:00:48.993 --> 00:00:52.463
Addition is associative: grouping two and four first,

00:00:52.463 --> 00:00:55.212
or four and one first, gives the same sum.

00:00:55.212 --> 00:00:57.699
Subtraction can change when regrouped.

00:00:58.004 --> 00:01:01.123
Sequential processing handles the pipeline without

00:01:01.123 --> 00:01:03.182
splitting it into parallel parts.

00:01:03.182 --> 00:01:06.364
Parallel stream processing can compute parts at the

00:01:06.364 --> 00:01:08.610
same time and combine their results.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.791
Our lambda is stateless: its result depends only on

00:01:11.791 --> 00:01:14.224
its inputs, not changing shared values.

00:01:14.224 --> 00:01:17.343
Noninterference means leaving the source unchanged

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:18.404
while processing.

00:01:18.404 --> 00:01:21.461
These rules support a correct reduction without a

00:01:21.461 --> 00:01:22.272
shared total.

00:01:22.272 --> 00:01:25.454
Encounter order does not promise the order in which

00:01:25.454 --> 00:01:26.826
parallel work happens.

00:01:26.826 --> 00:01:29.633
Splitting and combining add work, so parallel

00:01:29.633 --> 00:01:32.004
execution is not automatically faster.

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:33.777
Predict both printed lines.

00:01:33.777 --> 00:01:37.148
Start with the identity, combine all three counts, and

00:01:37.148 --> 00:01:40.331
decide whether splitting the work should change the

00:01:40.331 --> 00:01:40.705
total.

00:01:40.705 --> 00:01:41.392
Pause here.

00:01:46.241 --> 00:01:47.732
Both totals are seven.

00:01:47.732 --> 00:01:51.119
Zero adds nothing, and addition gives the same sum

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:52.271
under regrouping.

00:01:52.271 --> 00:01:55.659
The print statements display the sequential result

00:01:55.659 --> 00:01:58.165
first and the parallel result second.

00:01:58.165 --> 00:02:01.688
That display order does not show which parallel part

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:02.366
ran first.

00:02:02.691 --> 00:02:05.727
Add another four, then predict both totals.

00:02:05.727 --> 00:02:07.421
Next, try an empty list.

00:02:07.421 --> 00:02:11.021
How could you place filter and map before reduce to

00:02:11.021 --> 00:02:13.492
total only doubled positive counts?

00:02:13.492 --> 00:02:16.316
Explain why zero and addition still fit.
